Find data distributions using box plot and scatter plot
# Sample data
x <- c(10, 20, 30, 40, 50)
y <- c(15, 25, 35, 45, 55)

# Box plot
boxplot(x, main = "Box Plot", col = "lightblue")

# Scatter plot
plot(x, y, main = "Scatter Plot",
     xlab = "X Values", ylab = "Y Values",
     col = "red", pch = 19)


Explanation:
- boxplot() displays the data distribution.
- plot() displays the relationship between two variables.
- pch = 19 represents solid dots.

b) Find outliers using plot
# Sample data with an outlier
x <- c(10, 12, 15, 18, 20, 22, 100)

# Box plot
boxplot(x, main = "Outlier Detection", col = "lightgreen")

# Find outliers
outliers <- boxplot.stats(x)$out
print(outliers)


Output:
[1] 100


Explanation: The box plot identifies outliers as points beyond the whiskers. boxplot.stats(x)$out returns the detected outlier values.
c) Plot histogram, bar chart and pie chart on sample data

1. Histogram
x <- c(10, 20, 20, 30, 30, 30, 40, 50)

hist(x, main = "Histogram",
     xlab = "Values", col = "lightblue")


2. Bar chart
marks <- c(80, 90, 70)
names(marks) <- c("A", "B", "C")

barplot(marks, main = "Bar Chart",
        xlab = "Students", ylab = "Marks",
        col = "orange")


3. Pie chart
values <- c(30, 40, 20, 10)
labels <- c("A", "B", "C", "D")

pie(values, labels = labels,
    main = "Pie Chart",
    col = rainbow(4))


